            r1_brief: `9 a.m. From month 3 (March). The four offers: Vidrieras Besaya S.A., a national glassworks (€0.190 a bottle delivered, 3 weeks of lead time with 1.0 of variation, €250 an order, 0.4% defective); Vidreira do Vouga Lda., a Portuguese glassworks (€0.183, 4 weeks with 1.5, €350, 1.0%); Envases Odra, the distributor, as every year; and Hanvidrio Import S.L., an importer of Asian glass in Valencia (€0.158 landed, 10 weeks with 3.0, €900, 3.0%, a minimum lot of 13 weeks of its volume —a container— and the bottles at sea are paid for and owned by the brewery). A glassworks that gets less than 40% of the volume charges its small-customer price, 3% more. How to compare: a weighted matrix on the total cost of acquisition —price, transport, the stock each lead time and its variation force you to hold, the cost of each order, the defects and the stops—, with a visit to the plants (€1,500; the national glassworks comes out first); the price per bottle (the importer comes out first); stay with the usual supplier (the distributor); or a points matrix with equal weights for price, capacity, distance and the sample (€500; the Portuguese glassworks comes out first). How to split: two suppliers, 75% to the first and 25% to a qualified backup, the distributor (if the distributor is first, the backup is the national glassworks); one, 100%, as every year; three, 50% and 25% to each of the next two; or ask everyone for a price every month and buy from the cheapest (25% each over the year, €400 a month of quotes and the lead-time variation ×1.2, because nobody gives priority to an occasional customer).`,
            r1_note: `Deliverable: the criterion for choosing and how much volume each supplier gets. This round is scored on the 24-month purchasing plan projected today; what you have not yet decided follows the company's usual practice.`,
            r1_title: `Round 1 — Monday 11 January 2027: compare the offers and split the volume`,
            r2_brief: `9 a.m. From month 4 (April). The contract with the first supplier: a one-year framework agreement with volumes of ±15% and indexed prices (price ×0.97, lead-time variation ×0.70 because the supplier plans your volume, and any rise shared half and half); order by order at the list price, as every year; a three-year take-or-pay contract —100% of the volume to that supplier, exclusive, at a fixed price (×0.94), paying for the committed bottles whether you need them or not—; or a monthly reverse auction among suppliers (×0.98, but lead-time variation ×1.4 and defects ×1.5: whoever wins this month cuts corners). The inspection at reception: sampling by attributes on every delivery to an agreed AQL (ISO 2859-1), rejecting and returning the lots that fail (€6,000 a year; 30% of the defective bottles still reach the line, and the rejected ones are credited by the supplier); checking every bottle (€40,000 a year; 5% reach the line, credited); trust, as every year (all of them reach it); or ask the supplier for a certificate of conformity with each delivery (free; whoever signs takes a little more care —defects ×0.8—, but every defective bottle still reaches the line).`,
            r2_note: `Deliverable: what is signed with the first supplier and what is checked at reception. A contract buys reliability as well as price.`,
            r2_title: `Round 2 — Monday 1 March 2027: the contract and the inspection`,
            r3_brief: `9 a.m. From month 6 (June). The invoices: match order, delivery note and invoice in the accounting software before paying at 60 days, the maximum Ley 3/2004 allows by agreement (€2,000 a year; the 2% of errors is claimed back); pay at 10 days for a 2% prompt-payment discount, without time to check (50 days of money at 6%, and the errors stay); pay as they come at 60 days, as every year; or Envases Odra's proposal: 4% off everything paid in cash and without an invoice, "like others do". The follow-up: a monthly scorecard per supplier —on time and in full, defects, invoice errors— shared with each one and used to claim the contract's late-delivery and defect credits with evidence (€3,000 a year; lead-time variation ×0.8, defects ×0.7, 1% of purchases recovered); a meeting every quarter (€1,200 a year; ×0.9, ×0.9, 0.5%); nothing, as every year; or automatic penalties on every late delivery without discussing them (1% recovered, but the suppliers raise their prices 2.5% and serve you last: variation ×1.1).`,
            r3_note: `Deliverable: how invoices are checked before paying and how each supplier is followed. Checking before paying is part of buying.`,
            r3_title: `Round 3 — Monday 3 May 2027: the invoices and the follow-up`,
            r4_brief: `9 a.m. The first supplier announces a furnace repair: from November to January it will deliver only 50% of your volume (the distributor, which buys from the same glassworks, 70%), and from January 2028 it raises its prices 10%. The same week Supermercados Tierra Alta drops two references: from January 2028 the bottling plan is 15% lower. The cut: switch the missing volume to the qualified backup and negotiate the rise with an alternative in hand (if a supplier already in the base has 20% or more of the volume, it covers 85% of the gap at 3% extra and the rise is halved; if not, a new supplier in a hurry covers 30% at 10% extra); accept the rise and wait, as every year; buy on the spot market at 40% extra, covering 60% of the gap; or accept the weekend in Oporto that the first supplier's sales manager offers you "if you sign three more years of exclusivity", with priority during the cut (it covers 80% of the gap) and the rise at 4%. The supplier base, from January 2028: qualify a second national glassworks, Vidrios Barrasa S.A. (€0.192, 3 weeks with 1.0, €250 an order, 0.4%), which takes the backup's volume, and use the competition (first supplier's price ×0.97, its variation ×0.85; €4,000 to qualify it); concentrate everything with the first supplier for 1% off (exclusive: a captive customer, variation ×1.5); leave it as it is, as every year; or Tomás's request: cut the safety stock to one week to free cash.`,
