            voice_intro: `You are Alba Gormaz, 21, and you joined administration after the grado superior in Administración y Finanzas. Cervecera Revinuesa S.L., a 34-person brewery in Soria, bottles about 80,000 33 cl bottles a week (4,160,000 a year), with about 20,000 of weekly variation in the bottling plan. A missing bottle costs about €0.80 of beer margin that is not bottled; a defective bottle that reaches the line costs €1.00 in breakage, cleaning and stops. Today everything is bought from Envases Odra S.L., a distributor, at €0.220 a bottle (1.5 weeks of lead time with 0.8 of variation, €60 an order, 0.4% defective), order by order; nothing is inspected at reception; invoices are paid at 60 days as they arrive, without checking them (2% is lost in errors); and there are two weeks of bottling as safety stock. A year like this costs €978,305: 99.64% service to the line and 20.44 stock turns. Four offers are on the table. Julián Cidones, the managing director: "We pay the most expensive bottle in Spain. You decide, with numbers". Tomás Ucero, your boss, wants a lower price; Elena Abejar, the head of production, never wants to stop the line. It is judged over 24 months, from January 2027 to December 2028. The simulator works with expected values.`,
